/**
\defgroup distrib_negbin2 Negative binomial, quadratic variance
\ingroup distrib
\brief Discrete count distribution with mean \f$\mu > 0\f$ and variance \f$\mu + \mu^2/\theta\f$, with the log-mass in Loader's saddle-point form and its parameter derivatives to order six.

`laplace::distrib::negbin2_distrib` models an overdispersed count \f$y \in \{0, 1, 2, \dots\}\f$ by its
mean \f$\mu > 0\f$ and a dispersion \f$\theta > 0\f$, with variance \f$\mu + \mu^2/\theta\f$, quadratic
in the mean. As \f$\theta \to \infty\f$ at fixed \f$\mu\f$ it becomes the Poisson distribution of
the same mean (\ref distrib_poisson "distrib_poisson"), and at \f$\theta = 1\f$ the geometric
distribution of the same mean (\ref distrib_geometric "distrib_geometric").
The log-mass and its derivatives to order six are its own; the
derivatives of the cumulative functions, the expected information, the quantile and the random
numbers come from \ref distrib_generic "the generic routes".

## Classic form and reparametrization

Classically the negative binomial counts the failures \f$y\f$ before the \f$r\f$-th success in
independent trials of success probability \f$p\f$,
\f$\binom{y + r - 1}{y} p^r (1 - p)^y\f$, with mean \f$r(1-p)/p\f$ and variance \f$r(1-p)/p^2\f$; the
shape \f$r > 0\f$ need not be an integer. Taking \f$r = \theta\f$ and
\f$p = \theta/(\theta + \mu)\f$ gives the mean \f$\mu\f$ and the variance above, and the log-mass

\f[
\ell(\mu, \theta; y) = \log\Gamma(y + \theta) - \log\Gamma(\theta) - \log\Gamma(y + 1)
  + \theta \log\frac{\theta}{\theta + \mu} + y \log\frac{\mu}{\theta + \mu}.
\f]

Evaluated in that form it loses about \f$\log_{10} y\f$ digits: the three log-gamma terms are of the
size of \f$y \log y\f$ while the log-mass is of the size of \f$\log y\f$.

## Parameters, domain, and regularity

The struct sets `kind = distrib_kind::discrete` and `n_params = 2`; the descriptors of `mu` and
`theta` give the open domain \f$(0, \infty)\f$ and declare the log-mass and the cumulative functions
infinitely smooth in both, with no kink and a support that does not move. The support runs from
\f$0\f$ to \f$+\infty\f$, `center()` returns \f$\mu\f$ and `scale()` returns
\f$\sqrt{\mu + \mu^2/\theta}\f$. Derivatives are packed as `sym_tensor<2, K>`, whose component
\f$b\f$ holds \f$\partial^K \ell / \partial\mu^{K-b} \partial\theta^{b}\f$
(\ref distrib_core "distrib_core").

## Formulas and cancellation-free evaluation

Both negative binomial parametrizations share one form, Loader's saddle point, the one
the Poisson and the binomial distributions also use (Loader, 2002;
\ref distrib_poisson "distrib_poisson", \ref distrib_binomial "distrib_binomial").
With \f$\delta\f$ Stirling's error (\ref stirling "stirling"),
\f$\operatorname{bd}_0(a, b) = a \log(a/b) - a + b\f$ the unit deviance, and, for \f$y \ge 1\f$,
\f$b_1 = \mu (y + \theta)/(\theta + \mu)\f$, \f$b_2 = \theta (y + \theta)/(\theta + \mu)\f$ and
\f$\Delta = \theta (y - \mu)/(\theta + \mu)\f$, so that \f$b_1 + b_2 = y + \theta\f$,
\f$b_1 - y = -\Delta\f$ and \f$b_2 - \theta = \Delta\f$:

\f[
\ell(\mu, \theta; y) = \frac{1}{2}\log\frac{\theta}{2\pi y (y + \theta)}
  + \delta(y + \theta) - \delta(y) - \delta(\theta)
  - \operatorname{bd}_0(y, b_1) - \operatorname{bd}_0(\theta, b_2).
\f]

Every term is then of the size of the result at any count. At \f$y = 0\f$ the form carries
\f$\log y\f$ and the mass is \f$-\theta \operatorname{log1p}(\mu/\theta)\f$ instead.

A deviance is differentiated through \f$\psi_1(z) = z - \operatorname{log1p}(z)\f$, since
\f$\operatorname{bd}_0(a, b) = a\, \psi_1\!\big((b - a)/a\big)\f$. At the shape, where
\f$b_2 - \theta = \Delta\f$ always, this is a product of \f$\theta\f$ and one composition with
\f$z_2 = \Delta/\theta = (y - \mu)/(\theta + \mu)\f$, and \f$b_2\f$ is never formed. At the count,
\f$z = (b_1 - y)/y = -\Delta/y\f$ and the same composition serves for \f$|z| < 1/2\f$, where the
deviance is of the size of \f$z^2\f$; above \f$1/2\f$ the two closed tensors of
\f$\partial^\alpha b_1 - y\, \partial^\alpha \log b_1\f$ are used instead, because the composition's
alternating sum has terms far larger than the result once the count is far from the mean. The
rational functions of the parameters are built from closed formulas rather than from products:
\f$\theta/(\theta+\mu)\f$ and \f$\mu/(\theta+\mu)\f$ have
\f$\partial^{p+q} = (-1)^{p+q} (p+q-1)!\,(p\theta - q\mu)/(\theta+\mu)^{p+q+1}\f$ up to sign, one
exact numerator, so their derivatives in either parameter alone are single terms.

The cumulative function is the classical incomplete beta identity
\f$F(k) = I_p(\theta, k + 1)\f$ with \f$k = \lfloor y \rfloor\f$ and \f$p = \theta/(\theta + \mu)\f$,
taken from `laplace::math::ibeta`, `ibetac`, `log_ibeta` and `log_ibetac`
(\ref incomplete_beta "incomplete_beta"), each side computed directly so that the small one is
never a difference. Of the expected information, \f$I_{\mu\mu} = (\mu + \mu^2/\theta)^{-1}\f$ and
\f$I_{\mu\theta} = 0\f$ are known in closed form, and the test checks the generic sum against them;
\f$I_{\theta\theta}\f$ has no closed form here and comes from that sum.

## Accuracy and limits

Against the references of `tools/reference/distrib_negbin.py` (5397 rows: the log-mass from
symbolic derivatives of the definition evaluated at 400 and 500 bits, the cumulative functions
from `mpmath`'s own incomplete beta at 200 and 260 bits, each kept where the two precisions agree
to \f$2^{-80}\f$), in ulps of the largest component of the same order at the same point
(\ref accuracy_conditioned "the conditioning model"), with the test's bounds for orders 0 to 6:

| quantity | worst measured | test bound |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `logpmf` | 18.8 | 28 |
| `logpmf_dk`, orders 1 to 6 | 5.2 to \f$1.6 \times 10^3\f$ | 90, 2340, 23, 9.1, 7.8, 13.5 |
| `log_cdf` and its generic derivatives | 17.5 to \f$8.1 \times 10^3\f$ | 450, 26, 5640, 800, 1460, 8700, \f$1.2 \times 10^4\f$ |
| `log_ccdf` and its generic derivatives | 45.7 to 519 | 310, 69, 108, 254, 342, 780, 548 |

The peak of \f$1.6 \times 10^3\f$ ulp at order two sits at \f$(\mu, \theta, y) = (10^3, 10^6, 1)\f$,
where \f$\theta = \mu^2/y\f$ makes the exact
\f$\partial^2 \ell / \partial\mu^2 = -y/\mu^2 + (y+\theta)/(\theta+\mu)^2\f$ a zero to three digits:
a zero of the quantity asked for, not of the route. The derivatives of \f$\log F\f$ are at the level
of the summation route that produces them. The cumulative functions inherit the conditioning of
\f$I_p(\theta, k+1)\f$ in \f$p\f$, which costs \f$\theta\f$ ulp per ulp of \f$p\f$.

## Kokkos usage

Every member is marked `LAPLACE_HD`, so the same code runs on the host and inside a device kernel.

```cpp
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/negbin2.hpp>

void eval_negbin2(Kokkos::View<const double*> y, Kokkos::View<double*> out, double mu,
                  double theta) {
  const laplace::distrib::negbin2_distrib d{mu, theta};
  Kokkos::parallel_for("negbin2_d2", y.extent(0), KOKKOS_LAMBDA(int i) {
    out(i) = d.logpmf_d2(y(i))[0];  // d^2 log p / dmu^2
  });
}
```

## Members

- `mu`, `theta`: the mean and the dispersion.
- `logpmf()`, `logpmf_dk<K>()` and `logpmf_d1()` to `logpmf_d6()`: the log-mass and its parameter
  derivatives to order six.
- `cdf()`, `ccdf()`, `log_cdf()`, `log_ccdf()`: the cumulative functions through the regularized
  incomplete beta; `success_prob()` returns \f$\theta/(\theta + \mu)\f$.
- `support_lower()`, `support_upper()`, `center()`, `scale()`, `param_kinks()`: the support, the
  location and spread used by the generic routes, and the empty set of non-smooth points.

## References

- N. L. Johnson, A. W. Kemp and S. Kotz (2005), Univariate Discrete Distributions, Wiley Series in Probability and Statistics. https://doi.org/10.1002/0471715816
- C. Loader (2002), Fast and Accurate Computation of Binomial Probabilities, manuscript, Lucent Technologies, Murray Hill, NJ. https://www.r-project.org/doc/reports/CLoader-dbinom-2002.pdf
- A. Meurer and others (2017), SymPy: symbolic computing in Python, PeerJ Computer Science, 3, e103. https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj-cs.103
- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
- NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §5.11 Asymptotic Expansions (Gamma Function), https://dlmf.nist.gov/5.11
- NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §8.17 Incomplete Beta Functions, https://dlmf.nist.gov/8.17
*/

/**
\defgroup distrib_negbin1 Negative binomial, linear variance
\ingroup distrib
\brief Discrete count distribution with mean \f$\mu > 0\f$ and variance \f$\mu(1 + \theta)\f$, the same mass as the quadratic parametrization taken in variables that stay regular at its Poisson limit.

`laplace::distrib::negbin1_distrib` is the same distribution as
\ref distrib_negbin2 "the quadratic parametrization", written with a dispersion \f$\theta > 0\f$ for
which the variance \f$\mu(1 + \theta)\f$ is proportional to the mean. Its Poisson limit is
\f$\theta \to 0\f$. Nothing in its expected information is orthogonal: \f$I_{\mu\theta} \neq 0\f$, so
all three blocks come from the generic sum over the support.

## Classic form and reparametrization

From the classic shape \f$r\f$ and success probability \f$p\f$ of
\ref distrib_negbin2 "the quadratic parametrization", this one takes \f$r = \mu/\theta\f$ and
\f$p = 1/(1 + \theta)\f$, which gives the mean \f$\mu\f$, the variance \f$\mu(1 + \theta)\f$ and the
log-mass

\f[
\ell(\mu, \theta; y) = \log\Gamma\!\left(y + \frac{\mu}{\theta}\right)
  - \log\Gamma\!\left(\frac{\mu}{\theta}\right) - \log\Gamma(y + 1)
  - \frac{\mu}{\theta}\operatorname{log1p}(\theta) + y \log\frac{\theta}{1 + \theta}.
\f]

## Parameters, domain, and regularity

As in \ref distrib_negbin2 "the quadratic parametrization": both parameters are open on
\f$(0, \infty)\f$, the log-mass and the cumulative functions are infinitely smooth in them, the
support does not move and runs from \f$0\f$ to \f$+\infty\f$, `center()` returns \f$\mu\f$ and
`scale()` returns \f$\sqrt{\mu(1 + \theta)}\f$.

## Formulas and cancellation-free evaluation

The log-mass is the saddle-point form of \ref distrib_negbin2 "the quadratic parametrization" at
the shape \f$r = \mu/\theta\f$, and what this parametrization adds is which variables the
derivatives may be taken in. The shape itself is not one of them: differentiating
\f$r = \mu/\theta\f$ \f$K\f$ times in \f$\theta\f$ puts terms of the size of \f$\theta^{1-K}\f$ into
the chain rule while \f$\partial^K \ell / \partial\theta^K\f$ is of the size of one, so the
composition loses a relative \f$\theta^{K-1}\f$. Every quantity is therefore written as a rational
function of \f$(\mu, \theta)\f$ that is regular at \f$\theta = 0\f$:

\f[
u = \frac{y\theta}{\mu}, \qquad b_1 = \frac{y\theta + \mu}{1 + \theta} = y - \frac{y - \mu}{1 + \theta},
\qquad w_1 = \frac{y - \mu}{y\theta + \mu},
\f]

so that \f$(y + r)/r = 1 + u\f$ and the prefactor \f$\tfrac{1}{2}\log(r/(y+r))\f$ is
\f$-\tfrac{1}{2}\operatorname{log1p}(u)\f$, while \f$b_1\f$ has closed partials of one term each.
Because \f$b_2 = b_1/\theta\f$ and \f$w_2 = -\theta w_1\f$ exactly, the deviance at the shape is

\f[
\operatorname{bd}_0(r, b_2) = -b_1 w_1\, \psi(-\theta w_1), \qquad
\psi(v) = \frac{D(1 + v)}{v}, \qquad D(1 + v) = (1 + v)\operatorname{log1p}(v) - v,
\f]

a product with no \f$1/\theta\f$ left in it. The deviance at the count is the one of
\ref distrib_negbin2 "the quadratic parametrization" with these maps. At \f$y = 0\f$ the log-mass is
\f$-\mu\,(1 + \kappa(\theta))\f$ with \f$\kappa(\theta) = \operatorname{log1p}(\theta)/\theta - 1\f$,
which is of the size of \f$\theta\f$ as \f$\theta \to 0\f$ and loses nothing there. The difference of
Stirling's errors \f$\delta(y + r) - \delta(r)\f$ is taken as a function of \f$s = \theta/\mu\f$
above \f$r = 10\f$, where both arguments are in the range of the asymptotic series of
\ref stirling "stirling" and the difference is an explicit function of \f$s\f$; below \f$r = 10\f$
the shape is a regular variable and the composition is taken in it.

The cumulative function is \f$F(k) = I_p(r, k + 1)\f$ with \f$p = 1/(1 + \theta)\f$ formed directly
(\ref incomplete_beta "incomplete_beta"), and the derivatives of \f$\log F\f$ and
\f$\log(1 - F)\f$, the expected information and its derivatives, the quantile and the random numbers
come from \ref distrib_generic "the generic routes".

## Accuracy and limits

On the same references as \ref distrib_negbin2 "the quadratic parametrization", in ulps of the
largest component of the same order at the same point, with the test's bounds for orders 0 to 6:

| quantity | worst measured | test bound |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `logpmf` | 41.2 | 62 |
| `logpmf_dk`, orders 1 to 6 | 24.6 to \f$3.1 \times 10^3\f$ | 430, 37, 60, 4600, 660, 2000 |
| `log_cdf` and its generic derivatives | 39.3 to \f$9.0 \times 10^8\f$ | \f$8.1 \times 10^5\f$, 62, 59, \f$2.5 \times 10^4\f$, \f$4.0 \times 10^5\f$, \f$1.8 \times 10^7\f$, \f$1.4 \times 10^9\f$ |
| `log_ccdf` and its generic derivatives | 25 to 272 | 410, 43, 43, 38, 38, 82, 164 |

Three corners carry those worst figures. At \f$(\mu, \theta, y) = (10^5, 10^{-3}, 100158)\f$ the
four pieces of the assembled log-mass are 140 to 700 times the fourth derivative, which is where
the \f$3.1 \times 10^3\f$ ulp comes from. At \f$(10^3, 10^{-6})\f$ the success probability is
\f$1 - 10^{-6}\f$ and \f$\log I_p\f$ is within \f$10^{-6}\f$ of zero, where its relative error is
that of a logarithm near zero: \f$5.4 \times 10^5\f$ ulp on the value of \f$\log F\f$. At
\f$\theta = 1000\f$, a shape of \f$0.04\f$ whose tail decays by \f$0.999\f$ a step, the summation
route reaches \f$9.0 \times 10^8\f$ ulp at order six. On the two builds the measured figures agree
everywhere but two cells, where they differ by one ulp.

## Kokkos usage

```cpp
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/negbin1.hpp>

void eval_negbin1(Kokkos::View<const double*> y, Kokkos::View<double*> out, double mu,
                  double theta) {
  const laplace::distrib::negbin1_distrib d{mu, theta};
  Kokkos::parallel_for("negbin1_logpmf", y.extent(0), KOKKOS_LAMBDA(int i) {
    out(i) = d.logpmf(y(i));
  });
}
```

## Members

- `mu`, `theta`: the mean and the dispersion.
- `logpmf()`, `logpmf_dk<K>()` and `logpmf_d1()` to `logpmf_d6()`: the log-mass and its parameter
  derivatives to order six.
- `cdf()`, `ccdf()`, `log_cdf()`, `log_ccdf()`: the cumulative functions through the regularized
  incomplete beta; `shape()` returns \f$\mu/\theta\f$ and `success_prob()` returns
  \f$1/(1 + \theta)\f$.
- `support_lower()`, `support_upper()`, `center()`, `scale()`, `param_kinks()`: the support, the
  location and spread used by the generic routes, and the empty set of non-smooth points.

## References

- N. L. Johnson, A. W. Kemp and S. Kotz (2005), Univariate Discrete Distributions, Wiley Series in Probability and Statistics. https://doi.org/10.1002/0471715816
- C. Loader (2002), Fast and Accurate Computation of Binomial Probabilities, manuscript, Lucent Technologies, Murray Hill, NJ. https://www.r-project.org/doc/reports/CLoader-dbinom-2002.pdf
- A. Meurer and others (2017), SymPy: symbolic computing in Python, PeerJ Computer Science, 3, e103. https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj-cs.103
- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
- NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §5.11 Asymptotic Expansions (Gamma Function), https://dlmf.nist.gov/5.11
- NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §8.17 Incomplete Beta Functions, https://dlmf.nist.gov/8.17
*/
